    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.ai\n\n### Description\n\nAs someone coming from [nvim-treesitter-textobjects](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter-textobjects) I would expect `MiniAi.move_cursor()` function to accept one more additional jump variant as the first argument. Right now it's just `left` or `right` and the one I am missing is `closest` that would move cursor to either `left` or `right` depending on witch side is the closest to the current cursor position similar to what `nvim-treesitter-textobjects` provides, see [Text objects: move section](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter-textobjects?tab=readme-ov-file#text-objects-move), `]d` and `[d` mappings.\n\n### Use Case\n\nHere is my use case if you are interested. I find the default `g[<id>`/`g]<id>` mappings to be too much to type and they don't allow me to jump backwards. This is why I go with what `nvim-treesitter-textobjects` suggests: `]<id>`/`]<ID>` for jumping forward to the left and to the right side of a textobject respectively. One problem with this approach is that not every textobject is defined as a letter, some are a symbol (like \\` for markdown inline code sections is my case) which don't have upper case variant to distinguish right side jumps from left side jumps. In this case `MiniAi.move_cursor('closest', ...)` would come really handy.",
