        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Let's consider jumping with `search_method = 'next'` which would be a `]<id>` mapping. The `[<id>` will behave the same just in reverse.\n\nSuppose we have a text like this (`|` represents current cursor position):\n\n``` markdown\n|Lorem Ipsum is `simply` dummy `text` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\n```\n\nPressing `]<id>`would jump to the next **left** side of a textobject:\n\n``` markdown\nLorem Ipsum is |`simply` dummy `text` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\n```\n\nPressing `]<id>` again would jump to the next **right** side of a textobject:\n\n``` markdown\nLorem Ipsum is `simply|` dummy `text` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\n```\n\nPressing `]<id>` again would jump to the next **left** side (again) of a textobject:\n\n``` markdown\nLorem Ipsum is `simply` dummy |`text` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\n```\n\nAnd so on. See how we alternate between jumping to the left and to the right side of a textobjects:\n\n``` markdown\nLorem Ipsum is `simply` dummy |`text` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\nLorem Ipsum is `simply` dummy `text|` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\nLorem Ipsum is `simply` dummy `text` of the printing |`and` typesetting industry.\nLorem Ipsum is `simply` dummy `text` of the printing `and|` typesetting industry.\n```",
            "created_at": "2026-04-30T14:02:14Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/2398#issuecomment-4353101721",
