        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Pressing `]<id>` again would jump to the next **right** side of a textobject:\n> ```\n> Lorem Ipsum is `simply|` dummy `text` of the printing `and` typesetting industry.\n> ```\n\nUnfortunately, this won't work like this even with `side='closest'`. The `search_method = 'next'` first finds a `` ` `` textobject that is directly to the right of the cursor. This means that its start needs to be explicitly to the right of the cursor, which will find `` `text` ``. In this case its \"closest\" side is always \"left\".\n\nUsing the `search_method='cover_or_next'` with the suggested `side='closest'` will also not work as expected when the cursor is directly inside the textobject. The direction of the jump will depend on which half of the textobject region cursor is at. And this is the main reason why I don't really support the idea of this \"closest\" side.\n\nMy best suggestion would be to have only two `` ]` `` and `` [` `` and then resort to `` g[` `` and `` g]` `` to move to the other side of the current textobject (requires global search method to be `'cover'`).",
